# Never use stubs

Always deliver complete, working implementations. Do not use stubs.

- Never leave stub functions, placeholder bodies, or no-op implementations (e.g. `pass`, `return None`, `throw new Error("not implemented")`, `TODO`/`FIXME` in place of real logic).
- Never fake or hardcode data to simulate functionality that isn't actually implemented.
- Never comment out real logic and replace it with a placeholder to make something "look" done.
- If a full implementation depends on something genuinely unavailable (a missing secret, an external service, an undecided design), do not silently stub it — implement everything that can be built, and explicitly call out the blocker and what's needed to finish it.
- Prefer a smaller fully-working slice over a broad set of half-implemented stubs.
